it was a week of intrigue and anxiety as  kenyans waited for the presidential  results to be announced  william ruto was declared winner he got  about 7 million votes  his rival railrodinga came second and  immediately disputed the results  as odinga's supporters disrupted the  ceremony chaos broke out  several people were injured some said  they would not let the electoral  commission chair declare the winner  police were brought in to try and calm  the situation it has been a very tense  time both leaders have millions of  supporters so it's going to be  interesting to see how people's  expectations will be managed  seven electoral commissioners sit on the  board four of them have raised questions  about the integrity of the poor they  said they were dissatisfied by the  exercise but only the chair can declare  the winner  we  cannot take  ownership  of the results  that is going to be announced  because  of the opec nature  which these results have been handled  the sacrifices made when things ease up  the new president-elect spoke  striking a conciliatory tone saying he  will work with all kenyans i want to  promise  that  i will  work with all elected leaders  and all leaders in kenya  so that we can fashion a country  that leaves nobody behind  odinga's allies say the paul was not  free and fair many kenyans who support  him are disappointed we would want to  verify them once we verify them we will  be able to know and to tell the kenyan  people because  a result that is not verifiable  is not a result  odinga's next move will be to head to  the supreme court  he has seven days to file his petition  catherine soy al jazeera nairobi
 

